        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Implement group quantization. Instead of one scale per channel, use one scale per group of 128 weights within a channel. This is what GPTQ and AWQ actually use. Compare group sizes of 32, 64, 128, and 256 on the same weight matrix. Smaller groups give better quality but more storage overhead for scale factors.\n\n2. Build a mixed-precision quantizer. Quantize the first and last layers of a multi-layer network at INT8 while quantizing middle layers at INT4. Compare end-to-end output quality against uniform INT4 and uniform INT8. Measure the memory savings compared to all-INT8.\n\n3. Implement the straight-through estimator (STE) for quantization-aware training. Insert fake quantize/dequantize operations in the forward pass of a simple two-layer network trained on a regression task. Compare final loss between a model trained normally (then PTQ to INT4) versus a model trained with QAT from the start.\n\n4. Build an outlier-aware quantizer inspired by LLM.int8(). Detect channels where the activation magnitude exceeds 6x the mean. Keep those channels in FP16 and quantize everything else to INT8. Measure end-to-end quality on the transformer layer from Step 5 with varying outlier thresholds (3x, 6x, 10x).\n\n5. Implement a quantization quality dashboard. Given a weight matrix, compute and display: the weight distribution histogram, the quantization error distribution, per-channel scale factors, the worst-quantized channels (highest reconstruction error), and the cosine similarity between original and quantized outputs across 100 random inputs. Identify which channels should be kept at higher precision."
      ]
    }
